The Monod empirical model put forth by Monod22 has dominated the concept of microbial growth kinetics. The Monod model first presented the idea of a growth-limiting substrate. The Monod equation is given as \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
                                                  \usepackage{amsmath}
                                                  \usepackage{wasysym}
                                                  \usepackage{amsfonts}
                                                  \usepackage{amssymb}
                                                  \usepackage{amsbsy}
                                                  \usepackage{mathrsfs}
                                                  \usepackage{upgreek}
                                                  \setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
                                                  \begin{document}$$\:\mu\:={\mu\:}_{max}\frac{S}{Ks+S}$$\end{document}, where µ is specific growth rate (/h), µmax is maximum specific growth rate (/h), S is limiting substrate concentration in mg/l, and Ks is half-saturation constant in mg/l. Here, the equation is the fundamental framework for understanding the interaction between microbial growth and substrate consumption. Batch growth experiments were used to identify the kinetic parameters that describe the growth of free microbial culture using various COD and nitrate concentrations. The growth rate was investigated using initial COD and nitrate concentrations of 100 and 50 mg/L, respectively, which demonstrated a decent decrease rate. The Monod growth kinetics achieved for simultaneous COD and nitrate removal in the spouted bed reactor showed strong substrate-dependent microbial growth behaviour, confirming that the biological treatment procedure adhered to traditional Monod kinetics. For both COD and nitrate systems, the plots of specific growth rate (µ) versus substrate concentration demonstrated a slow rise in microbial growth rate as substrate availability increased. The observed (R2) values of 0.9046 for COD and 0.9778 for nitrate from Fig. 4 represent good linearity, showing a strong agreement between experiments and the Monod kinetic model. The higher values of R show consistent biological growth characteristics during the course of reactor operation, with proper utilisation of substrate by the bacteria23.
